---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "@components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import LeadForm from "@components/LeadForm.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "@components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import { BRAND } from "~/lib/brand";
import { serviceLinks } from "~/lib/internal-links";

// NEEDS OWNER: specific written workmanship-warranty term (years/coverage) —
// stated on estimates today; publish here once Eric confirms the standard term.
// See TASKS_FOR_ERIC.md. Never invent a number.
---

<BaseLayout
  seo={{
    title: "Roof Warranties, Explained Honestly",
    description:
      "What roof warranties actually cover: manufacturer tiers, what any contractor can register, what requires certification — and what Northvale puts in writing.",
    pathname: "/warranty",
  }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-10">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Warranty" }]} />
    <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl md:text-5xl">Roof warranties, explained honestly</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg text-[color:var(--color-ink-800)]">
      Warranty talk is where roofing sales gets slippery. Here's how the coverage actually works —
      including which tiers require certifications a contractor must hold, and where we stand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-4 py-12 md:grid-cols-[1fr_400px]">
    <article class="prose-northvale">
      <h2>Two different warranties protect a roof</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The manufacturer's warranty</strong> covers the product — defects in the shingles and
        system components. <strong>The workmanship warranty</strong> covers the installation — the thing
        that actually causes most premature failures. Any honest conversation keeps them separate.
      </p>

      <h2>IKO coverage: what we register on every job</h2>
      <p>
        We standardize on IKO, so the warranty story is simple and consistent — every roof gets the
        same coverage, registered to you at close:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Limited Lifetime product warranty</strong> against manufacturing defects on IKO Dynasty
          and Nordic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Iron Clad Protection — 15 years, non-prorated.</strong> The first 15 years are full
          coverage with no depreciation math, right through the window when a manufacturing defect is
          most likely to appear. It's coverage any installer can register — no special certification required.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>130-mph limited high-wind warranty</strong> on Dynasty and Nordic when installed to
          IKO's application requirements — which is how we nail every roof.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>10-year algae-resistance warranty</strong> against the blue-green streaking Gulf-Coast
          humidity causes.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One honest note IKO makes plainly: the Class 3 (Dynasty) and Class 4 (Nordic) impact ratings
        are UL 2218 lab classifications, <em>not</em> a guarantee against hail — hail damage isn't covered
        by the shingle warranty. The rating's value is helping you qualify for an insurance premium discount
        where your carrier offers one.
      </p>

      <h2>The extended tiers — and where we stand</h2>
      <p>
        IKO reserves its longest coverage — <strong>Iron Clad Extended Protection</strong>, which
        can run up to 25 years depending on shingle type — for contractors at the higher <strong
          >ROOFPRO</strong
        >
        membership tiers. <strong>{BRAND.certStatus || "We are an IKO ROOFPRO applicant"}</strong> — and
        until that membership is confirmed, we will not advertise or sell the extended coverage that depends
        on it. When that changes, this page changes. Your estimate always states the exact warranty for
        your job in writing.
      </p>

      <h2>Our workmanship warranty</h2>
      <p>
        Every Northvale estimate states the workmanship warranty for that job in writing — term and
        coverage, on paper, before you sign anything. No verbal "don't worry, we'll take care of
        you." If a written promise isn't in the estimate, it doesn't exist; ours are.
      </p>

      <h2>What voids warranties (and how we protect yours)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Bad installation</strong> — off-spec nailing voids wind coverage; we nail to manufacturer
          spec, six-nail where wind exposure calls for it, and photograph the work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Poor attic ventilation</strong> — manufacturers can reduce or deny coverage over it,
          and ARMA's 140°F-attic numbers explain why. We size ventilation on every replacement.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pressure washing</strong> — ARMA warns it damages asphalt roofing. Algae streaks have
          gentler answers, including the 10-year algae-resistance warranty that comes on the IKO shingles
          we install.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unregistered warranties</strong> — some coverage requires registration; we complete
          it at close-out and hand you the confirmation in the documentation packet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Questions about coverage on a roof we didn't install? Bring us the paperwork during a
        <a href="/services/roof-inspection"> free inspection</a> and we'll tell you what you actually
        have.
      </p>
    </article>
    <aside class="md:sticky md:top-10 md:self-start">
      <LeadForm source="warranty-page" />
    </aside>
  </section>

  <RelatedLinks heading="Our services" links={serviceLinks()} />
</BaseLayout>
